meta {
  name: delete without the write scope
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

delete {
  url: {{base_url}}/v1/documents/{{document_id}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{search_api_key}}
}

docs {
  AD-024, and the reason it exists.

  `search_api_key` is the key the n8n workflow holds. That workflow processes
  scraped, untrusted content, so a prompt injection that reaches its credential
  must not be able to purge the corpus. Attribution alone would tell you
  afterwards which key did it; the scope is what stops it happening.

  The failure is a `403` naming the missing scope — deliberately more
  informative than the `401` above it. A caller reaching here already holds a
  valid key and already knows it exists, so telling it which scope it lacks is
  diagnosable rather than leaky.

  Set `search_api_key` in the environment to a key configured with
  `KB_API__API_KEY_SCOPES=<key_id>:search`. Skip this request if the deployment
  has only one key.
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
  res.body.code: eq insufficient_scope
  res.body.required_scope: eq write
}
